<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The gates opened at ten; by five past, the Bubble Police found their kick-and-vilify training\'97in which they had drilled for forgers, fence-jumpers, and agitators with wire cutters and grievances\'97severely lacking. In the face of thousands of people bearing tickets to a lawfully staged festival, they abandoned their posts. in part because they were Cloudborne themselves, but mainly because they were mostly rockabilly fans already, and were offered free tickets. Plus the Elvi were signing autographs and offering advice on which cocktails to avoid at the refreshment stands.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Drugs?\'94 asked a Bubble Constable hopefully, as he returned his pistol under the waistband of his jeans and the tired elastic of his underwear. He\'92d left the safety catch off, ever presently at risk of becoming one sausage short of a barbie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes,\'94 an Elvine answered. \'93Cane sugar and peanut butter.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I mean, who doesn\'92t love an Elvi song?\'94 was a common response as the Bubble Police clasped their shiny-bright tickets.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 They came down from their residential towers and up from everywhere else. As the crowd swelled, identifying Airborne versus  Cloudborne versus Elvi, became impossible, which was rather the point. The Airborne young dressed for the ground the way their trust fund patrons (who, in other cultures, were commonly referred to as 
\f2\i parents
\f1\i0 ) dressed for the opera\'97with research, and a stylist on retainer. They stepped onto actual grass with the tentative rapture of astronauts on a beach; several were seen touching it.  Some of the already munted picked blades of it and chewed. The Cloudborne dressed in whatever glittered. The Elvi were a minority at their own festival, and many of them had volunteered to help marshal, bless, and direct foot traffic with karate hands. For everyone who could not be on the lawn, a live stream carried the day out to every Elvi in The Pale and the wider Ulyssian Meritocracy, and a great many of the uncloned besides.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Around the perimeter of the lawn, merch tents sold t-shirts that glittered like mirror balls, capes in all sizes, ponchos guaranteed against rain the Bubble made meteorologically impossible, pompadour wigs, and stick-on sideburns with adhesive that was already negotiating release by noon. One item on sale was priced for Airborne allowance accounts only:  the exclusive, collectible Rock Against Revival Air Guitar. It came in its own festival-branded soft case\'97emblazoned with the tag, \'93thank you very much,\'94 allegedly in the hand of the High Elvis himself\'97which turned out to contain nothing more than stickers, a bag of plectrums, and enough pumped-in air to inflate the case to guitar proportions. When a button marked 
\f3\b\fs18 UNLOCK
\f1\b0\fs22  on the side was pressed, the case released its air through six pipes (a deluxe edition had twelve) that collectively wheezed a low, flatulent, magnificent drop D power chord. The Airborne young flocked to the merch tables, recognising exclusivity when they saw it: a personal item comfortably priced higher, gram for gram, than Eastern Suburbs real estate, but with one significant improvement over bricks and mortar. It came with a strap.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Rock Against Revival Air Guitar sold out by lunchtime. The Airborne kids, far from feeling ripped off, laughed at the joke, strapped the bag over their shoulders, and went about the event playing air guitar at everyone that didn\'92t have one. One especially enterprising Airborne kid had an air guitar derivative instrument registered on the nation\'92s commodity exchange by close of business.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bar tents ran to theme. The Blue Howahya entry-level offering; beyond it the menu climbed through the Chunk o\'92 Churning Lava Butterscotch and the Cadillac Malt to a thing called Love Me Condensed, which was served in a plastic boot. What the drinks had in common was a structural reliance on banana, peanut butter, condensed milk and a bacon-salt rim that made each one less a cocktail than a dessert with a moat. The Cloudborne kids, raised on whatever was going, handled them fine. The Airborne kids\'97raised at altitude on optimised macro-nutritious slushies invented by life-style influencers\'97had no antibodies for any of it, and ordered seconds.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The first band, The Tupelo Trio, opened with a tune that in a straighter universe would have answered to \'93That\'92s All Right\'94, and the sound system, admitting the truth about itself for the first time, thumped the opening chord through half a million sternums at once. The surrounding fig trees gave up their entire holding of fruit bats in one black exhalation, and every picnic rug on the lawn stood up with its owners attached. The Domain had heard louder things in its time\'97such as the 1812 Overture back when the cannon were real, and that Funeral-Gabber DJ duo that we can\'92t name for legal reasons and threats they\'92d play our homes to rubble\'97but it had never heard anything this loud and this happy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The trouble arrived with the second act, and the trouble was the words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Four lines into a song all of you in more stately universes would recall and instantly taps your toes to, three Elvi came from stage-left at a dead sprint to argue about the merits of the lyrics with karate. The crowd roared with delight as  frontman toppled into the photographers\'92 pit and his sunglasses went into the fourth row. The crew rescued the microphones first and the singer second, and the band resume playing\'97for about sixteen bars until the fight broke out again. A roadie hauled the frontman clear by the collar but, by that time, the set was a wash. The stage manager consulted her clip board for the next-up act and shot a look towards the green room that would have curdled a Love Me Condensed in its own boot.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The squabbling became a pattern into the afternoon. Some acts even surrendered their time on stage by punching on amongst themselves. In the marquee behind the stand that served as the green room, bands with the Teacher\'92s words ate on the left, bands intending to freelance ate on the right, but even that didn\'92t stop fights breaking out, although combatants possessed enough civility to hang up their capes first, because there was justifiable violence and then there was dry-cleaning. Trays of fried sandwiches fell to the grass as grown Elvi, often  in complementary jumpsuits, rolled under trestles, throwing short punches and quoting mondegreens as if they were scripture. Past the rider tables, at the marquee\'92s far end, a ring of Elvisettes and a general atmosphere of exemption marked where the Chanta, the Great Teacher himself was sitting the whole question out. Nobody threw a punch nor rolled anywhere near him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The size of the bands increased from three- to four-pieces; then they had keyboards then their first horn; by mid afternoon a band with backing singers and a man whose entire job was tambourine, and the day warmed to the temperature the drinks had been waiting for. Airborne teenagers who had taken on too many cocktails and too much sun  folded neatly at the tree line to give back the condensed milk and peanuts, felled by sweetness and saturated fat in an environment their bodies had never been consulted about. The St Johns volunteers, who had prepared for crush injuries and misadventure, worked through the afternoon on a casualty profile their incident forms had no box for, and settled, form after form, on 
\f2\i allergic reaction
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The punch-ups, meanwhile, continued. Who could say who forgot what lyrics and who chose to chance their own? Every deviation emptied the side-stages; festival goers climbed up and fought with bouncers and musicians alike. The crowd loved the double act of concert and fight club, scored each stage invasion with the discrimination of accumulating expertise, and booed the sound crew whenever the microphones came away too promptly. Deep in the middle of the lawn, a few hundred Airborne kids mounted a punch-up of their own: slow-motion, pulled karate, a boy in a certified authentic poncho selling a knockout so operatic that strangers carried him overhead like a fallen king while his friends fanned him with Air Guitar cases. The fighting was, as far as they could tell, included in the ticket, which itself was a license to participate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time a band with a full brass section opened with three lead singers who decided to sing their own lyrics over the top of one another, the whole green room emptied\'97apart from Chanta and a few of his more devoted Elvisettes\'97and joined the on-stage riot. The horn section defended its riser like a hill fort, still playing while they kicked assailants down. Elvi were punching up in the crowd and Airborne kids quickly started running books on the outcomes. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then the High Elvis walked out. There was no fanfare, which on that stage, on that day, was itself a special effect. He came on at a stroll, in a suit that was doing none of the shouting, stepped around two colleagues wrestling on the stage deck, and picked a fallen microphone off the boards. The sound engineer, seeing who was on stage, pulled front-of-house. The back-stage fold-back engineer did the same. The High Elvis tapped the mic until he heard the sound of it echoing across The Domain like a gravel-throated metronome. He kept tapping, as patient as a claims assessor, while the punch-up around him became, fist by fist, self-conscious. The crowd fell quiet, apart form the Airborne kids throwing up at the tree line.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis recited the Oath of the Warranty, which solemnised the Elvi while baffling everyone else.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Everyone here loves the music,\'94 The High Elvis then said. \'93Why else would we be here? But we Elvi need to set a good example for our younger fans, so it seems we need to lay a little law down. I hereby decree three new laws for Elvi to abide by, and for our friends and fans to observe as a mark of solidarity and respect.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All the jumbotrons around the arena lit up with the new Elvi laws. And then The High Elvis read them aloud.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 1: Sing the words of The Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 2: Do not punch up on anyone who does not sing the words of the Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Number 3: Sing the words of The Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you very much.\'94
